{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NkswqwfRf8HsvXTXZLjGpN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"OpenTelemetry project (CNCF)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Observability\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"observability\", \"signals\", \"traces\", \"metrics\", \"logs\", \"baggage\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# OpenTelemetry — Signals (opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/)\n9\t\n10\tSignals are system outputs that describe the underlying activity of the operating system and applications running on a platform. A signal can be something you want to measure at a specific point in time, like temperature or memory usage, or an event that goes through the components of your distributed system that you'd like to trace.\n11\t\n12\tOpenTelemetry currently supports:\n13\t- **Traces** — the path of a request through your application.\n14\t- **Metrics** — a measurement captured at runtime.\n15\t- **Logs** — a recording of an event.\n16\t- **Baggage** — contextual information that is passed between signals.\n17\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"ed0a7d9c-8c6f-42c6-a3fb-985b2fbfb523","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:47:59.896Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/opentelemetry-signals-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"OpenTelemetry project (CNCF)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Observability\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"observability\", \"signals\", \"traces\", \"metrics\", \"logs\", \"baggage\"]\n---\n# OpenTelemetry — Signals (opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/)\n\nSignals are system outputs that describe the underlying activity of the operating system and applications running on a platform. A signal can be something you want to measure at a specific point in time, like temperature or memory usage, or an event that goes through the components of your distributed system that you'd like to trace.\n\nOpenTelemetry currently supports:\n- **Traces** — the path of a request through your application.\n- **Metrics** — a measurement captured at runtime.\n- **Logs** — a recording of an event.\n- **Baggage** — contextual information that is passed between signals.\n","numLines":17,"startLine":1,"totalLines":17}}}
