{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J5dgY6ghGkKxmJnHphmNmW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://www.jaegertracing.io/docs/latest/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:40:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Jaeger project (CNCF graduated)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Observability\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"jaeger\", \"distributed-tracing\", \"tracing-backend\", \"opentracing\", \"opentelemetry-compatible\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Jaeger (jaegertracing.io/docs/latest/)\n9\t\n10\tJaeger is a distributed tracing platform released as open source by Uber Technologies in 2016 and donated to Cloud Native Computing Foundation where it is a graduated project. With Jaeger you can: monitor and troubleshoot distributed workflows; identify performance bottlenecks; track down root causes; analyze service dependencies. Jaeger was originally designed to support the OpenTracing standard. OpenTelemetry provides APIs and SDKs in multiple languages to allow applications to export various telemetry data, while the Jaeger project is primarily the tracing backend that receives tracing telemetry data and provides processing, aggregation, data mining, and visualizations. Jaeger is OpenTelemetry compatible; terminology and concepts map directly between the two data models.\n11\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"ee43b24a-5051-41b3-91fb-6ec087577151","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:48:00.556Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/jaeger-overview-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://www.jaegertracing.io/docs/latest/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:40:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Jaeger project (CNCF graduated)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Observability\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"jaeger\", \"distributed-tracing\", \"tracing-backend\", \"opentracing\", \"opentelemetry-compatible\"]\n---\n# Jaeger (jaegertracing.io/docs/latest/)\n\nJaeger is a distributed tracing platform released as open source by Uber Technologies in 2016 and donated to Cloud Native Computing Foundation where it is a graduated project. With Jaeger you can: monitor and troubleshoot distributed workflows; identify performance bottlenecks; track down root causes; analyze service dependencies. Jaeger was originally designed to support the OpenTracing standard. OpenTelemetry provides APIs and SDKs in multiple languages to allow applications to export various telemetry data, while the Jaeger project is primarily the tracing backend that receives tracing telemetry data and provides processing, aggregation, data mining, and visualizations. Jaeger is OpenTelemetry compatible; terminology and concepts map directly between the two data models.\n","numLines":11,"startLine":1,"totalLines":11}}}
