{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wq6q5GrSXK6SwHYL1L7nNA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://docs.fluentbit.io/manual/about/what-is-fluent-bit\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:40:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Fluent Bit project (Fluent, CNCF graduated)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Observability\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"fluent-bit\", \"fluentd\", \"log-processor\", \"telemetry-agent\", \"data-pipeline\", \"input\", \"parser\", \"filter\", \"buffer\", \"router\", \"output\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Fluent Bit (docs.fluentbit.io)\n9\t\n10\tFluent Bit is an open source telemetry agent that processes logs, metrics, traces, and profiles. Fluent Bit was created in 2014 by Eduardo Silva as a lightweight log processor, developed by the Fluentd team at Treasure Data for constrained environments such as embedded Linux; it is a sub-project of Fluentd, which is a CNCF graduated project. Both are commonly deployed on Kubernetes as node-level logging agents (DaemonSets) that collect container logs from each node and forward them to a backend.\n11\t\n12\t## Data pipeline\n13\tThe Fluent Bit data pipeline incorporates several specific concepts. Data processing flows through the pipeline following these concepts in order: **Input** — input plugins gather information from different sources; some plugins collect data from log files, and others gather metrics information from the operating system. **Parser** — parsers convert unstructured data to structured data. **Filter** — filters let you alter the collected data before delivering it to a destination. **Buffer** — the buffering phase provides a unified and persistent mechanism to store your data, using the primary in-memory model or the file system-based mode. **Router** — routing lets you route your data through filters, and then to one or multiple destinations; the router relies on the concept of tags and matching rules. **Output** — output plugins let you define destinations for your data; common destinations are remote services, local file systems, or other standard interfaces.\n14\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"6ea51c7c-9399-436f-ad96-831eda86c5f9","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:48:03.620Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/fluent-bit-overview-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://docs.fluentbit.io/manual/about/what-is-fluent-bit\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:40:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Fluent Bit project (Fluent, CNCF graduated)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Observability\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"fluent-bit\", \"fluentd\", \"log-processor\", \"telemetry-agent\", \"data-pipeline\", \"input\", \"parser\", \"filter\", \"buffer\", \"router\", \"output\"]\n---\n# Fluent Bit (docs.fluentbit.io)\n\nFluent Bit is an open source telemetry agent that processes logs, metrics, traces, and profiles. Fluent Bit was created in 2014 by Eduardo Silva as a lightweight log processor, developed by the Fluentd team at Treasure Data for constrained environments such as embedded Linux; it is a sub-project of Fluentd, which is a CNCF graduated project. Both are commonly deployed on Kubernetes as node-level logging agents (DaemonSets) that collect container logs from each node and forward them to a backend.\n\n## Data pipeline\nThe Fluent Bit data pipeline incorporates several specific concepts. Data processing flows through the pipeline following these concepts in order: **Input** — input plugins gather information from different sources; some plugins collect data from log files, and others gather metrics information from the operating system. **Parser** — parsers convert unstructured data to structured data. **Filter** — filters let you alter the collected data before delivering it to a destination. **Buffer** — the buffering phase provides a unified and persistent mechanism to store your data, using the primary in-memory model or the file system-based mode. **Router** — routing lets you route your data through filters, and then to one or multiple destinations; the router relies on the concept of tags and matching rules. **Output** — output plugins let you define destinations for your data; common destinations are remote services, local file systems, or other standard interfaces.\n","numLines":14,"startLine":1,"totalLines":14}}}
