{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017z3nBNQHqvG321ULUkJoPj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/metrics-server/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-31T06:20:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Kubernetes SIGs (kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server), the reference implementation of the Metrics API\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D3.1\", \"D2.3\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"metrics-server\", \"components-yaml\", \"kubectl-apply-f\", \"aggregation-layer\", \"declarative-install\", \"metrics-api\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# metrics-server — how it is installed (kubernetes-sigs.github.io/metrics-server/)\n9\t\n10\t> **Snapshot note.** Fetched 2026-08-31 to close Chapter 14's G-14d, which asked whether\n11\t> metrics-server's *composition* could be sourced. **It cannot, from this page.** The page does\n12\t> not enumerate the objects inside `components.yaml` — no Deployment, Service, ClusterRole,\n13\t> ServiceAccount or APIService is named. What it does state is the installation *shape*, which\n14\t> is the fact Chapter 14 actually argues from: installation is `kubectl apply -f` against a file\n15\t> of objects somebody else wrote.\n16\t>\n17\t> **Do not use this snapshot to support an object-by-object enumeration.** Ch 14's earlier\n18\t> \"a Deployment, a Service, RBAC rules, an APIService registration\" phrasing is NOT supported\n19\t> here and was narrowed at the 2026-08-31 integration gate. Closing `components.yaml` itself\n20\t> would require caching a release artifact, which is a heavier and more perishable citation than\n21\t> this chapter needs.\n22\t\n23\t## Installation\n24\t\n25\t> \"Latest Metrics Server release can be installed by running:\"\n26\t\n27\t```\n28\tkubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\tA Helm chart is referenced as an alternative install path; the page gives no object\n32\tspecifications for it either.\n33\t\n34\t## What it is, and what it requires\n35\t\n36\t> \"Metrics Server collects resource metrics from Kubelets and exposes them in Kubernetes\n37\t> apiserver through Metrics API\"\n38\t\n39\t> \"kube-apiserver must enable an aggregation layer\"\n40\t\n41\t## Not on this page\n42\t\n43\tThe individual objects contained in `components.yaml` are not described. The page covers\n44\trequirements, use cases and configuration guidance rather than deployment composition.\n45\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"bafc53b6-3036-4e2a-87fa-0278c05ce450","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:48:32.689Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/metrics-server-install-2026-08-31.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/metrics-server/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-31T06:20:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes SIGs (kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server), the reference implementation of the Metrics API\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D3.1\", \"D2.3\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"metrics-server\", \"components-yaml\", \"kubectl-apply-f\", \"aggregation-layer\", \"declarative-install\", \"metrics-api\"]\n---\n# metrics-server — how it is installed (kubernetes-sigs.github.io/metrics-server/)\n\n> **Snapshot note.** Fetched 2026-08-31 to close Chapter 14's G-14d, which asked whether\n> metrics-server's *composition* could be sourced. **It cannot, from this page.** The page does\n> not enumerate the objects inside `components.yaml` — no Deployment, Service, ClusterRole,\n> ServiceAccount or APIService is named. What it does state is the installation *shape*, which\n> is the fact Chapter 14 actually argues from: installation is `kubectl apply -f` against a file\n> of objects somebody else wrote.\n>\n> **Do not use this snapshot to support an object-by-object enumeration.** Ch 14's earlier\n> \"a Deployment, a Service, RBAC rules, an APIService registration\" phrasing is NOT supported\n> here and was narrowed at the 2026-08-31 integration gate. Closing `components.yaml` itself\n> would require caching a release artifact, which is a heavier and more perishable citation than\n> this chapter needs.\n\n## Installation\n\n> \"Latest Metrics Server release can be installed by running:\"\n\n```\nkubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml\n```\n\nA Helm chart is referenced as an alternative install path; the page gives no object\nspecifications for it either.\n\n## What it is, and what it requires\n\n> \"Metrics Server collects resource metrics from Kubelets and exposes them in Kubernetes\n> apiserver through Metrics API\"\n\n> \"kube-apiserver must enable an aggregation layer\"\n\n## Not on this page\n\nThe individual objects contained in `components.yaml` are not described. The page covers\nrequirements, use cases and configuration guidance rather than deployment composition.\n","numLines":45,"startLine":1,"totalLines":45}}}
