{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M2mpNBLsLbrj4Fehcrb8Tu","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pushgateway: Key Definitions and Guidelines\n\n**(1) What the Pushgateway is:**\n\"The Pushgateway is an intermediary service which allows you to push metrics from jobs which cannot be scraped.\"\n\n**(2) When it should be used:**\n\"The only valid use case for the Pushgateway is for capturing the outcome of a service-level batch job.\"\n\n**(3) Warning about limited use cases:**\n\"We only recommend using the Pushgateway in certain limited cases.\"\n\n**(4) Service-level batch job definition:**\n\"A 'service-level' batch job is one which is not semantically related to a specific machine or job instance.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"26a16ea0-339b-48f5-9329-da12224c0a26","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:49:29.913Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":155922,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Pushgateway: Key Definitions and Guidelines\n\n**(1) What the Pushgateway is:**\n\"The Pushgateway is an intermediary service which allows you to push metrics from jobs which cannot be scraped.\"\n\n**(2) When it should be used:**\n\"The only valid use case for the Pushgateway is for capturing the outcome of a service-level batch job.\"\n\n**(3) Warning about limited use cases:**\n\"We only recommend using the Pushgateway in certain limited cases.\"\n\n**(4) Service-level batch job definition:**\n\"A 'service-level' batch job is one which is not semantically related to a specific machine or job instance.\"","durationMs":2561,"url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/pushing/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016e3cHmRESmh2qohc9xGs9t","type":"tool_result","content":"# Prometheus Labels Section - Key Quotes\n\n**(1) New time series from label combinations:**\n\"Every unique combination of key-value label pairs represents a new time series, which can dramatically increase the amount of data stored.\"\n\n**(2) High cardinality warning:**\n\"Do not use labels to store dimensions with high cardinality (many different label values), such as user IDs, email addresses, or other unbounded sets of values.\"\n\n**(3) Guidance on label usage:**\n\"Do not put the label names in the metric name, as this introduces redundancy and will cause confusion if the respective labels are aggregated away.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"9b61b23a-7492-4b7a-98f6-fa201dc98093","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:49:30.241Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":205106,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Prometheus Labels Section - Key Quotes\n\n**(1) New time series from label combinations:**\n\"Every unique combination of key-value label pairs represents a new time series, which can dramatically increase the amount of data stored.\"\n\n**(2) High cardinality warning:**\n\"Do not use labels to store dimensions with high cardinality (many different label values), such as user IDs, email addresses, or other unbounded sets of values.\"\n\n**(3) Guidance on label usage:**\n\"Do not put the label names in the metric name, as this introduces redundancy and will cause confusion if the respective labels are aggregated away.\"","durationMs":2061,"url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/practices/naming/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0168hcEJFDrC14YEwBGshAdb","type":"tool_result","content":"# Observability Definitions and Distinctions\n\n**(1) Observability:**\n\"a measure of how well internal states of a system can be inferred from knowledge of its external outputs.\"\n\n**(2) Monitoring:**\n\"a system that can detect known unknowns\" (as contrasted with observability in the document).\n\n**(3) Difference between monitoring and observability:**\nThe document states: \"Monitoring, traditionally, was a system admin or human operator (ops) concern\" where \"ops folks had to infer the state of the system from external signals,\" whereas observability emphasizes \"being able to find and reason about unknown unknowns as well.\"\n\n**(4) Known unknowns vs. unknown unknowns:**\n\"Monitoring is called a system that can detect known unknowns -- as opposed to observability which emphasizes being able to find and reason about unknown unknowns as well.\"\n\n**(5) Primary signals enumerated:**\nThe document identifies: metrics, logs, traces, profiles, and dumps. It notes regarding these five: \"they are commonly mentioned and probably what you're going to start with.\"\n\n**RED and USE methods:**\n\"the [USE method](https://www.brendangregg.com/usemethod.html)\" and \"the [RED method](https://www.weave.works/blog/the-red-method-key-metrics-for-microservices-architecture/)\" are both mentioned but not directly quoted or defined in the provided text."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"a9581f7a-d9af-4b1a-b9a9-5e91bd645038","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:49:31.032Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":562401,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Observability Definitions and Distinctions\n\n**(1) Observability:**\n\"a measure of how well internal states of a system can be inferred from knowledge of its external outputs.\"\n\n**(2) Monitoring:**\n\"a system that can detect known unknowns\" (as contrasted with observability in the document).\n\n**(3) Difference between monitoring and observability:**\nThe document states: \"Monitoring, traditionally, was a system admin or human operator (ops) concern\" where \"ops folks had to infer the state of the system from external signals,\" whereas observability emphasizes \"being able to find and reason about unknown unknowns as well.\"\n\n**(4) Known unknowns vs. unknown unknowns:**\n\"Monitoring is called a system that can detect known unknowns -- as opposed to observability which emphasizes being able to find and reason about unknown unknowns as well.\"\n\n**(5) Primary signals enumerated:**\nThe document identifies: metrics, logs, traces, profiles, and dumps. It notes regarding these five: \"they are commonly mentioned and probably what you're going to start with.\"\n\n**RED and USE methods:**\n\"the [USE method](https://www.brendangregg.com/usemethod.html)\" and \"the [RED method](https://www.weave.works/blog/the-red-method-key-metrics-for-microservices-architecture/)\" are both mentioned but not directly quoted or defined in the provided text.","durationMs":5835,"url":"https://github.com/cncf/tag-observability/blob/main/whitepaper.md"}}
