{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QX58EWNAkXYjWDaVQ6agkf","type":"tool_result","content":"# Alertmanager Overview\n\n**(1) What it is and what it does:**\n\"The Alertmanager handles alerts sent by client applications such as the Prometheus server. It takes care of deduplicating, grouping, and routing them to the correct receiver integration such as email, PagerDuty, or OpsGenie. It also takes care of silencing and inhibition of alerts.\"\n\n**(2) Which component sends alerts to it:**\nAccording to the documentation, \"client applications such as the Prometheus server\" transmit alerts to the Alertmanager.\n\n**(3) Core functions performed:**\n\n- **Grouping:** \"Grouping categorizes alerts of similar nature into a single notification.\"\n\n- **Inhibition:** \"Inhibition is a concept of suppressing notifications for certain alerts if certain other alerts are already firing.\"\n\n- **Silencing:** \"Silences are a straightforward way to simply mute alerts for a given time.\"\n\n- **Routing:** The system directs notifications to \"the correct receiver integration such as email, PagerDuty, or OpsGenie.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"226f174d-99ab-4fab-b055-c17a568fd132","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:50:00.748Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":162936,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Alertmanager Overview\n\n**(1) What it is and what it does:**\n\"The Alertmanager handles alerts sent by client applications such as the Prometheus server. It takes care of deduplicating, grouping, and routing them to the correct receiver integration such as email, PagerDuty, or OpsGenie. It also takes care of silencing and inhibition of alerts.\"\n\n**(2) Which component sends alerts to it:**\nAccording to the documentation, \"client applications such as the Prometheus server\" transmit alerts to the Alertmanager.\n\n**(3) Core functions performed:**\n\n- **Grouping:** \"Grouping categorizes alerts of similar nature into a single notification.\"\n\n- **Inhibition:** \"Inhibition is a concept of suppressing notifications for certain alerts if certain other alerts are already firing.\"\n\n- **Silencing:** \"Silences are a straightforward way to simply mute alerts for a given time.\"\n\n- **Routing:** The system directs notifications to \"the correct receiver integration such as email, PagerDuty, or OpsGenie.\"","durationMs":3489,"url":"https://prometheus.io/docs/alerting/latest/alertmanager/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V1euujtLssRnt3H91dHKTA","type":"tool_result","content":"# OpenTelemetry Instrumentation: Key Definitions\n\n**(1) Definition of Instrumentation:**\n\"Code from the system's components must emit [signals], such as [traces], [metrics], and [logs].\" This process of emitting these signals is what constitutes instrumentation.\n\n**(2) Code-based vs. Zero-code Instrumentation:**\n\n**Code-based:** \"Allow you to get deeper insight and rich telemetry from your application itself\" by using the OpenTelemetry API to generate telemetry directly from your code.\n\n**Zero-code:** \"Great for getting started, or when you can't modify the application you need to get telemetry out of\" and \"provide rich telemetry from libraries you use and/or the environment your application runs in.\"\n\n**(3) Observable Systems Requirement:**\n\"For a system to be observable, it must be instrumented: that is, code from the system's components must emit signals.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"b6dd81eb-8410-4e5b-8fe6-b056b3095ac9","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:50:01.530Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":2998,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# OpenTelemetry Instrumentation: Key Definitions\n\n**(1) Definition of Instrumentation:**\n\"Code from the system's components must emit [signals], such as [traces], [metrics], and [logs].\" This process of emitting these signals is what constitutes instrumentation.\n\n**(2) Code-based vs. Zero-code Instrumentation:**\n\n**Code-based:** \"Allow you to get deeper insight and rich telemetry from your application itself\" by using the OpenTelemetry API to generate telemetry directly from your code.\n\n**Zero-code:** \"Great for getting started, or when you can't modify the application you need to get telemetry out of\" and \"provide rich telemetry from libraries you use and/or the environment your application runs in.\"\n\n**(3) Observable Systems Requirement:**\n\"For a system to be observable, it must be instrumented: that is, code from the system's components must emit signals.\"","durationMs":2730,"url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/instrumentation/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EpEaG1RxGMFDyBvq1CYLf9","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"RED method rate errors duration Tom Wilkie official documentation site\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The RED Method: How to Instrument Your Services | Grafana Labs\",\"url\":\"https://grafana.com/blog/the-red-method-how-to-instrument-your-services/\"},{\"title\":\"The RED Method Patterns for instrumentation & monitoring. Tom Wilkie\",\"url\":\"https://grafana.com/files/grafanacon_eu_2018/Tom_Wilkie_GrafanaCon_EU_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Grafana dashboards: A complete guide to all the different types you can build | Grafana Labs\",\"url\":\"https://grafana.com/blog/grafana-dashboards-a-complete-guide-to-all-the-different-types-you-can-build/\"},{\"title\":\"Grafana dashboard best practices | Grafana documentation\",\"url\":\"https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/best-practices/\"},{\"title\":\"Observability - Monitoring\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/r3code/917219f186c1697ffa865279c3292c04\"},{\"title\":\"Monitoring - Observability\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/StevenACoffman/836295e378dbb3e2d9bc1dac074086ad\"},{\"title\":\"Grafana dashboard best practices | Grafana Cloud documentation\",\"url\":\"https://grafana.com/docs/grafana-cloud/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/best-practices/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - charlax/professional-programming: A collection of learning resources for curious software engineers · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/charlax/professional-programming\"},{\"title\":\"tomwilkie (Tom Wilkie) · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/tomwilkie\"},{\"title\":\"A Practical Guide: From Instrumenting Code to Specifying Alerts with the RED Method\",\"url\":\"https://www.weave.works/blog/a-practical-guide-from-instrumenting-code-to-specifying-alerts-with-the-red-method\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found comprehensive information about the RED method and Tom Wilkie's documentation on it:\n\n## The RED Method Overview\n\nTom Wilkie created the RED Method in 2015. Tom came up with the RED Method after a new employee asked what his monitoring philosophy was.\n\n## The Three Components\n\nThe RED Method focuses on three key metrics: everyone should understand the error rate, the request rate, and then some distribution of latency for those requests.\n\nMore specifically:\n- (Request) Errors - the number of failed requests per second\n- (Request) Duration - The amount of time each request takes expressed as a time interval\n\nThe method also tracks request rate (Rate), giving it the name \"RED.\"\n\n## Purpose and Benefits\n\n\"The USE Method doesn't really apply to services; it applies to hardware, network disks, things like this,\" Tom said. \"We really wanted a microservices-oriented monitoring philosophy, so we came up with the RED Method.\"\n\nYou model this for every single service in your architecture, and this gives you a nice, consistent view of how your architecture is behaving. Giving this kind of consistency across services allows you to scale your operational team, and allows you to put people on call for code they didn't write.\n\n## Official Documentation\n\nFor more information, refer to Tom Wilkie's blog post The RED method: How to instrument your services, which is available on Grafana's official documentation and blog sites.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1daab9a-2140-4147-8700-50292188b384","uuid":"ae7a0c8d-ec2a-47fa-9b38-bc75c8121fa2","timestamp":"2026-08-31T20:50:03.413Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"RED method rate errors duration Tom Wilkie official documentation site","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01PEXZqkaHsCdmUV7ZWdV7gk","content":[{"title":"The RED Method: How to Instrument Your Services | Grafana Labs","url":"https://grafana.com/blog/the-red-method-how-to-instrument-your-services/"},{"title":"The RED Method Patterns for instrumentation & monitoring. Tom Wilkie","url":"https://grafana.com/files/grafanacon_eu_2018/Tom_Wilkie_GrafanaCon_EU_2018.pdf"},{"title":"Grafana dashboards: A complete guide to all the different types you can build | Grafana Labs","url":"https://grafana.com/blog/grafana-dashboards-a-complete-guide-to-all-the-different-types-you-can-build/"},{"title":"Grafana dashboard best practices | Grafana documentation","url":"https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/best-practices/"},{"title":"Observability - Monitoring","url":"https://gist.github.com/r3code/917219f186c1697ffa865279c3292c04"},{"title":"Monitoring - Observability","url":"https://gist.github.com/StevenACoffman/836295e378dbb3e2d9bc1dac074086ad"},{"title":"Grafana dashboard best practices | Grafana Cloud documentation","url":"https://grafana.com/docs/grafana-cloud/visualizations/dashboards/build-dashboards/best-practices/"},{"title":"GitHub - charlax/professional-programming: A collection of learning resources for curious software engineers · GitHub","url":"https://github.com/charlax/professional-programming"},{"title":"tomwilkie (Tom Wilkie) · GitHub","url":"https://github.com/tomwilkie"},{"title":"A Practical Guide: From Instrumenting Code to Specifying Alerts with the RED Method","url":"https://www.weave.works/blog/a-practical-guide-from-instrumenting-code-to-specifying-alerts-with-the-red-method"}]},"Based on the search results, I found comprehensive information about the RED method and Tom Wilkie's documentation on it:\n\n## The RED Method Overview\n\nTom Wilkie created the RED Method in 2015. Tom came up with the RED Method after a new employee asked what his monitoring philosophy was.\n\n## The Three Components\n\nThe RED Method focuses on three key metrics: everyone should understand the error rate, the request rate, and then some distribution of latency for those requests.\n\nMore specifically:\n- (Request) Errors - the number of failed requests per second\n- (Request) Duration - The amount of time each request takes expressed as a time interval\n\nThe method also tracks request rate (Rate), giving it the name \"RED.\"\n\n## Purpose and Benefits\n\n\"The USE Method doesn't really apply to services; it applies to hardware, network disks, things like this,\" Tom said. \"We really wanted a microservices-oriented monitoring philosophy, so we came up with the RED Method.\"\n\nYou model this for every single service in your architecture, and this gives you a nice, consistent view of how your architecture is behaving. Giving this kind of consistency across services allows you to scale your operational team, and allows you to put people on call for code they didn't write.\n\n## Official Documentation\n\nFor more information, refer to Tom Wilkie's blog post The RED method: How to instrument your services, which is available on Grafana's official documentation and blog sites."],"durationSeconds":7.56794750000001,"searchCount":1}}
